Fix: pqNTRUSign reference challenge() message-length truncation typo.

Both sign() and verify() in common/pqNTRUSign.c hardcode a message length of
32 bytes when calling challenge(), instead of passing the real `msg_len`
argument that crypto_sign / crypto_sign_open already thread all the way down
(crypto_sign -> sign(sig, m, mlen, ...), crypto_sign_open -> verify(sig, m,
*mlen, ...)). As written, only the first 32 bytes of any message are
authenticated: two messages sharing a 32-byte prefix produce interchangeable
signatures -- a trivial selective-forgery avenue. The official specification
(Algorithms 2-5 in Supporting_Documentation/pqNTRUsign.pdf) hashes the full
message mu in {0,1}*; the 32 is neither HASH_BYTES (=64, SHA-512) nor any other
meaningful constant, so this is an implementation typo, not the intended
design, and is not mentioned in the official NIST-forum comments. Fix = pass
the real msg_len at both call sites.

Applied to the build copy (common/), never the vendored archive -- same
convention as the NTT.c fix in build_cli.sh.

--- common/pqNTRUSign.c
+++ common/pqNTRUSign.c
@@ -282,7 +282,7 @@
     a2 = 0;
     a3 = 0;

-    challenge (sptp, h, msg, 32, param);
+    challenge (sptp, h, msg, msg_len, param);
 /*    int total = 0;
     for (i=0;i<param->N*2;i++)
         total = total+sptp[i];
@@ -410,7 +410,7 @@
     int64_t *sptp   = v     + param->padded_N;    /* 2 polynomials */
     int64_t *buffer = sptp  + param->padded_N*2;

-    challenge (sptp, h, msg, 32, param);
+    challenge (sptp, h, msg, msg_len, param);
